  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 359 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\n\nScattered convection initializing across the eastern CO/WY and the \nwestern NE border this afternoon will be closely watched as they \ntrack east/southeast through the rest of the evening and nighttime \nhours. Though most of these storms will likely be in the stages of \ndecay by the time they are approaching our western fringe (around 9PM \nto 1AM), the potential for a stronger storm to make it into the area \nis not entirely zero. The locations most likely receive such storms \nwould be for areas mainly concentrated south and west of the Tri-\nCities. A similar scenario may play out Monday night as well, though \nthe focus may be shifted more northward (best potential north and \nwest of the Tri-Cities).  \n\nGiven strong, yet elevated instability tonight and Monday (up to \n2,000-5,000J/kg of MUCAPE mainly from the contribution of strong mid-\nlevel lapse rates: 8-10C/km), a few of the larger/taller storms \ncould use this \"energy\" to be able to maintain through a portion of \nthe night. The main inhibitor that keeps the chance for widespread \nstorms and mainly the severe potential more limited, however, would \nbe from the presence of weaker shear aloft (20-35kts; generally \ndecreasing through the night). In addition to the weak shear aloft, \nthe nocturnal low-level jet is also projected to lie just east of \nthe area (given the placement of the low-level low pressure center), \nlimiting the potential for further convective initiation. With all that \nbeing said, an isolated severe storm, mainly Monday night, can't be \nruled out of the realm of possibilities even though the overall \npotential appears to be more questionable at this time. \n\nFor the forth night in a row, fog remains a strong possibility \novernight into the morning hours on Monday. The latest HREF guidance \ncontinues to have higher than usual confidence for fog potential as \n70% of the ensemble members resolve fog with 50% of them showing \npatches of dense fog. Virtually the same locations this morning \n(areas near and north of I-80) could be targeted again given the \nreturn of similar conditions (light to calm winds with temperatures \nand dewpoints approaching the mid 60s).  \n\nThe heat will return Monday (upper 80 and 90s) and Tuesday (90s to \n104 degrees). Heat index values both days should range the mid 90s \nto 105 degrees. The greatest heat will generally fall across north \ncentral Kansas. These locations will have the best potential to see \nheat index values over 100 degrees as well as the greatest chances \nfor temperatures to reach/exceed 100 degree (20-40% chance MON & 40-\n90% chance TUE). The only factor that has been theorized to knock \nhigh down by a few degrees recently and may continue to do so over \nthe next few days has been the presence of elevated smoke. Evident \non satellite and from the hazy skies, a few more plumes of elevated \nsmoke may continue to pass overtop given the northwesterly flow \nregime we find the area underneath.  After Tuesday, a cloudier and \nwetter (possible stormy) pattern begins to emerge in the forecast. \nHighs as a result may reach the 70s again by Friday (60-90% chance \nfor south central Nebraska & 40-60% chance for north central \nKansas). \n\nThe upper-level flow has been gradually returning back to a more \nzonal (horizontal: west to east) flow. This pattern is known to be \nfairly unstable as passing shortwave disturbances can be more \nsubtle. Daily storm initiation tends to be more reliant on mesoscale \nfeatures that can change more rapidly from day to day (in comparison \nto more predictable synoptic-level forcings). As result of this more \nunsettled pattern we find the area in, forecast confidence tends to \nfall more on the lower-end of the spectrum. Looking ahead at the \ntrends for the end of the week, the National Blend of Models \n(NBM) is suggesting a more stormy pattern where daily \nthunderstorm chances return each day. At this point in time, the\nlevel of details that we can provide remain limited given the \nhigher than usual model uncertainty and lack of consistency \nbetween model runs.",
